WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.500
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.790
This work is about using AI to create super
detailed smoke simulations.

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:14.959
Typically, creating a crude simulation doesn't
take very long, but as we increase the resolution,

00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:17.960
the execution time and memory consumption
skyrockets.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:23.980
In the age of AI, it only sounds logical to
try to include some learning algorithms in

00:00:23.980 --> 00:00:24.980
this process.

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:30.640
So what if we had an AI-based technique that
would have some sort of understanding of smoke

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.769
simulations, take our crude data and add the
fine details to it?

00:00:34.769 --> 00:00:39.870
This way, we could obtain a high resolution
smoke simulation without waiting several days

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.840
or weeks for the computation.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.589
Now if you are a truly seasoned Fellow Scholar,
you may remember an earlier work by the name

00:00:46.589 --> 00:00:51.359
Wavelet Turbulence, which is one of my favorite
papers of all time.

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:56.109
So much so that it got the distinction of
being showcased in the very first Two Minute

00:00:56.109 --> 00:00:57.109
Papers episode.

00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:01.989
I was a sophomore college student back then
when I've first seen it and was absolutely

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:04.680
shocked by the quality of the results.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:07.160
That was an experience I'll never forget.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.910
It also won a technical Oscar award and it
is not an overstatement to say that this was

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:17.460
one of the most influential works that made
me realize that research is my true calling.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.480
The link to the first episode is available
in the video description and if you want to

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:24.670
see how embarrassing it is, make sure to check
it out.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:30.210
It did something similar, but instead of using
AI, it used some heuristics that describe

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:36.230
what is the ratio and distribution of smaller
and bigger vortices in a piece of fluid or

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.230
smoke.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:41.410
Using this information, it could create a
somewhat similar effect, but ultimately, that

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:46.520
technique had an understanding of smoke simulations
in general, but it didn't know anything about

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.290
the scene that we have at hand right now.

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:54.770
Another work that is related to this is showing
a bunch of smoke simulation videos to an AI

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.830
and teach it to continue these simulations
by itself.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:02.670
I would place this work as a middle ground
solution, because this work says that we should

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:07.060
take a step back and not try to synthesize
everything from scratch.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:12.780
Let's create a database of simulations, dice
them up into tiny tiny patches, look at the

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:18.590
same footage in low and high resolutions,
and learn how they relate to each other.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.129
This way, we can hand the neural network some
low resolution footage and it will be able

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:27.980
to make an educated guess as to which high
resolution patch should be the best match

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:28.980
for it.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:33.049
When we found the right patch, we just switch
the coarse simulation to the most fitting

00:02:33.049 --> 00:02:35.469
high-resolution patch in the database.

00:02:35.469 --> 00:02:40.439
You might say that in theory, creating such
a Frankenstein smoke simulation sounds like

00:02:40.439 --> 00:02:41.569
a dreadful idea.

00:02:41.569 --> 00:02:45.430
But have a look at the results, as they are
absolutely brilliant!

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:50.650
And as you can see, it takes a really crude
base simulation and adds so many details to

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.439
it, it's truly an incredible achievement.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:59.469
One neural network is trained to capture similarities
in densities, and one for vorticity.

00:02:59.469 --> 00:03:04.489
Using the two neural networks in tandem, we
can take a low resolution fluid flow and synthesize

00:03:04.489 --> 00:03:08.569
the fine details on top of it in a way that
is hardly believable.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:13.480
It also handles boundary conditions, which
means that these details are correctly added

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:16.450
even if our smoke puff hits an object.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.079
This was an issue with Wavelet Turbulence
which had to be addressed with several followup

00:03:21.079 --> 00:03:22.079
works.

00:03:22.079 --> 00:03:26.829
There are also comparisons against this legendary
algorithm, and as you can see, the new technique

00:03:26.829 --> 00:03:27.989
smokes it.

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:30.231
However, it took 9 years to do this.

00:03:30.231 --> 00:03:35.230
This is exactly 9 eternities in the world
of research, which is a huge testament to

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.159
how powerful the original algorithm was.

00:03:38.159 --> 00:03:42.389
It is also really cool to get more and more
messages where I get to know more about you

00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:43.389
Fellow Scholars.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:48.620
I was informed that the series is used in
school classes in Brazil, it is also used

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:53.870
to augment college education, and it is a
great topic for fun family conversations over

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:54.870
dinner.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.709
That's just absolutely fantastic.

00:03:56.709 --> 00:04:00.439
Loving the fact that the series is an inspiration
for many of you.

00:04:00.439 --> 00:04:04.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

